
# Why Should We Not Keep Large JavaScript Code Inside HTML?

## The Problem

Imagine your website has 10 pages.

If you write the same JavaScript code inside each HTML page:

- The browser downloads the JavaScript every time it downloads the HTML page.
- The same code gets repeated on multiple pages.
- If you need to make a change, you may have to update several HTML files.
- The HTML file becomes large and difficult to read.

---

## Why Use a Separate JavaScript File?

Example:

<script src="app.js"></script>

When the browser downloads app.js:

1. It stores the file in cache.
2. When another page uses the same file, the browser checks the cache first.
3. If the file hasn't changed, it uses the cached version.
4. This makes the website load faster and saves bandwidth.

---

## What Are Hash Names?

Normally, a JavaScript file may be named:

app.js

In modern applications, it may look like:

app.a4f7c2.js

After a code change:

app.b8k9d1.js

Why?

- The changed filename tells the browser that a new version exists.
- The browser downloads the new file.
- If the filename stays the same, the browser can continue using the cached version.

---

## Can We Write JavaScript Inside HTML?

Yes, absolutely.

Example:

<script>
    console.log("Hello");
</script>

This works perfectly fine.

However:

- The JavaScript becomes part of the HTML file.
- It cannot be cached separately.
- The same code may be downloaded repeatedly.
- The code becomes harder to maintain as the project grows.

---

## Advantages of Using Separate .js Files

✅ Better caching

✅ Faster page loading

✅ Reusable code across multiple pages

✅ Easier maintenance

✅ Cleaner and more organized code

✅ Better scalability for large projects

---

## Conclusion

Writing JavaScript inside <script> tags is fine for:

- Small scripts
- Learning purposes
- Quick testing

For real-world applications, it is recommended to keep JavaScript in separate .js files because it improves performance, maintainability, and code organization.